# Clean Architecture Rules

Architecture is a modular clean architecture implementation.

1. Modules are under /lib/features and are primarily DDD driven
2. Modules must define an entrypoint via module_name.api.dart
3. All modules must talk to other modules through the \*.api.dart entry point
4. All files inside a module must use relative imports to one another, use package imports to the \*.api.dart for accessing other modules
5. Respect folder structure and naming conventions laid out below

## Folder Structure

```
/module_name
    /data
        /mappers
        /converters
        /sources
        /repos
        /models
    /domain
        /entities
        /value_types
        /repos
        /services
        /failures
        /usecases
        /analytics
    /ui
        /features
            /feature1
        /widgets
        /routers
    /module
    module_name.api.dart
```

## Naming Conventions

- Mappers use domain_entity_name.mapper.dart
- Repositories use \*.repo.dart in the domain layer, and impls use \*.repo.impl.dart
- DTOs use \*.dto.dart
- Entities are just \*.dart
- Pages are \*.page.dart
- Controllers are \*.controller.dart
- Routers are \*.router.dart
- Data sources are \*.datasource.local.dart or \*.datasource.remote.dart

Class Implementations should be ClassNameImpl with Impl on the end

6. Follow our clean architecture rules below

## Clean Architecture specifics

### Data Layer

- Data layer is the infra layer, it is responsible for implementing repos, data sources, mappers, and models such as dtos or cache items
- Mappers are extensions on the dto or domain model, `toDomain()` and `toDto()`
- Use Freezed for all dto and cache model generation
- Local datasources should use the hive cache service with expirations
- Exceptions should be thrown (or mapped to more detailed exceptions) from datasource and caught in the repository, i.e. JumpNetworkException - and then mapped to Failure types.
- Each module should implement its own failure subclass, like OrdersFailure, then other Failures should be a sealed class of this so it can be switch() cased. If you have many and need more detailed failures, AuthFailure > LogoutFailure extends AuthFailure > LogoutFailure.sessionNotFound as a sealed class
- Log happy and sad paths in repositories with the `name: ""` field just being the module name
- Streams must use rxdart behavior subjects instead of StreamControllers

### Domain Layer

- Domain layer is standard, but this folder also encompasses the "app" layer of use cases and services
- UseCases are sometimes classes like final class DeleteItemUseCase {}
- UseCases can also be stream providers wrapping repo streams directly in the module layer if they are simple, like repo.watchCurrentOrder;
- No logging should happen in use cases unless indirectly triggering domain events. Logging should be in the presentation and infra layers
- All entities should have an extension file called `entity_name.analytics.dart that is an extension with a `toAnalyticAttributes` method or getter. This is essentially a toJson but in the domain layer for making logging share params across logs

### UI Layer

- UI layer aka presentation
- It uses riverpod notifiers as viewmodels, called "controllers" with immutable state classes and copy with for state updates.
- No repository or use case calls should leak into views, these should be handled in the controller.
- \*.router.dart is a simple interface and impl that can be defined at ui/routers layer (if basic) and be shared across features, or defined in each ui feature folder.
- Routers/coordinators only handles routing, called by the controller, this is so we can mock it and reduce side effects when testing controllers.
- Always try to use components from JumpUI library first, do not create new components in JumpUI without permission

### Module Layer

- This is a glue layer that assembles all ports and adapters, provided in a module_name.di.dart and module_name.internal.di.dart
- Providers are defined statically in a namespaced abstract class like ModuleNameDI or ModuleNameUseCases or ModuleNameInternalDI
- Public DI is exported through the \*.api.dart file
- Internal DI are repos or any assembled services
- This layer can also define classes / services that listen to events / state updates that come from other modules and call domain use cases or services, like NewOrderHandler or something that calls printOrderUseCase
- Do not export or statically define datasources, they should be constructed internally inside the repository's DI definition.
- Do not pass Riverpod Ref down to repositories for DI, inject them in the module layer.